  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 144, 145 and PR #1045 are merged into this branch; re-read the resolver as it now stands. Message the word-add-in-r1 session (census, #1034).</step>
    <step order="1">Census: sweep `Sprk.Bff.Api`, `Spaarke.Dataverse`, `Spaarke.Core` for every create, upsert-that-can-create and parent-changing update of a user-facing record (entity-name literals, `new Entity(...)`/`DataverseEntity(...)`, `PostAsync` to an entity set, `UpsertAsync`, `UpdateRecordFieldsAsync` with a new id). Classify each: child-of-core-record (fix) / root record (144's) / per-user by design (waiver, reason) / system-audit (waiver, reason) / dead code (delete or waiver). Start from peer note 080 §6.5 and the background list; record the table in the task note.</step>
    <step order="2">Derive each child table's parent-lookup set from live metadata for all three roots (OneToMany relationships of sprk_project, sprk_matter, sprk_workassignment — design.md §5.1d did project only). Record it.</step>
    <step order="3">Extend the resolver: accept the child's full parent-lookup values; secure-if-any rule → task 144's named team; flagged-secure-but-not-isolated parent → refuse; otherwise existing record-first behaviour (first resolvable parent's BU default team, then acting user). Cover with resolver unit tests including the both-project-lookups case.</step>
    <step order="4">Wire every writer classified "fix" to pass its parent values and set `ownerid` from the answer, refusing in its own contract. Include reparent updates: when an update changes a parent lookup, re-resolve and reassign the owner in a separate assign (never folded into the field PATCH — ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:606-608 rationale) and verify by read-back.</step>
    <step order="5">Add `RecordOwnerAssignmentCensusTests`: fails when a create/upsert of a listed user-facing entity in the BFF does not route an owner from the resolver and is not on the waiver list; waivers carry a reason. Seed a removal and watch it fail.</step>
    <step order="6">Integration tests per writer family: secure parent → named team; ordinary parent → its BU default team; both-project-lookups document with only `sprk_relatedproject` secure → named team; refusal writes nothing; fault propagates; unfiled document with an acting user still saves.</step>
    <step order="7">Update the I-2 and I-6 rows of DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md. Build, unit + integration + ArchTests; publish size and CVE per §10.</step>
    <step order="8" mode="prescriptive">MANUAL LIVE GATE (after deploy to dev, together with 149): with an existing non-admin test user in their own BU, create through each writer family a child of a secure project, a secure matter and a secure work assignment; record owner (read-back), and that a non-sharee test user cannot read it in MDA. Delete probes and record the deletion.</step>
    <step order="9">Update TASK-INDEX.md (this task's cell `✅ [done]`) and the POML status; close #1034 with the outcome (coordinated); write the task note including deviations.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>PR #1045 is not merged when this task starts: STOP before touching RecordOwnershipResolver.cs or the Office writers; do not fork the peer's changes.</trigger>
    <trigger>Record threads (`sprk_communicationthread`) regarding a secure parent: the peer classifies threads as per-user (owner = caller) while the owner's rule says every child of a secure record is secure. Surface both options (thread owned by the secure team + participant shares, vs caller-owned with the thread's regarding columns treated as a disclosure) per CLAUDE.md §6 before changing ThreadResolver. RECOMMENDATION: a RECORD thread whose regarding is a secure root is a child of that root and follows the secure-if-any owner rule with task 149's sharee mirror; the per-user MASTER thread (no regarding) stays caller-owned. Rationale: the thread's subject/regarding columns disclose the secure record exactly as a communication does.</trigger>
    <trigger>A child whose parent lookups name TWO different roots with conflicting securability semantics beyond "any secure → secure" (e.g. two different secure roots whose sharee sets differ): implement secure-if-any and escalate the sharee-set question to task 149's owner decision rather than choosing silently.</trigger>
    <trigger>A non-secure sprk_workassignment (or other root with its own `sprk_issecure`) filed under a secure project: whether it must become secure is an owner decision — surface it; do not re-own a ROOT from this task. RECOMMENDATION (owner to confirm): design.md §5.1d lists sprk_workassignment as a child of sprk_project, and the owner's C10 rule is "every child of a secure record must be secure" — so a work assignment under a secure project should be provisioned secure through task 144's generalized provisioning endpoint (never by a bare re-own here), and its creation under a secure project should do so inline.</trigger>
    <trigger>A run-as-user writer (EmailDraftToolHandler, DataverseCreateRecordHandler, or any census writer that creates as the caller) cannot set `ownerid` to a team in the Secure Record BU because the caller's role lacks `prvAssign` on that table at the needed depth: do NOT widen user roles and do NOT fall back to a create-then-app-assign that leaves the row user-owned in an ordinary BU for any interval without owner acceptance. Present the options (app-only create with the human recorded in a field — note task 152's Created By dependency — vs create-then-assign with its window) per CLAUDE.md §6.</trigger>
    <trigger>Task 152 cannot be deployed with or before this task (see the deployment-ordering constraint): surface the interim Daily Briefing drop-out of re-owned to-dos to the owner before deploying; do not ship silently.</trigger>
    <trigger>Any writer cannot refuse without breaking a contract the owner fixed (e.g. inbound email must never be dropped): record the conflict and surface it (§6.5 path A/B/C) instead of weakening the refusal.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`IRecordOwnershipResolver` (`Services/Dataverse/RecordOwnershipResolver.cs:59-117`) is the owner invariant's single server owner; it has zero callers here (grep `ResolveOwningTeamAsync` over src/ → only :66 and :144). No census of owner-less creates exists (grep over tests/Spaarke.ArchTests for "owner" → `SessionOwnershipGuardTests.cs` only, which guards sessions, not Dataverse creates).</existing>
    <extension>Yes for the owner logic: extend `RecordOwnershipContext` and `ResolveOwningTeamAsync` in place; the writers only become callers. The only new files are one ArchTest (the census #1034 asks for, in the `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests` shape) and one integration test file — no new service, endpoint, DI registration or package.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Every document, communication, event, to-do, analysis and invoice a server writer creates under a secure project, matter or work assignment is owned in an ordinary BU (usually root) and readable in MDA by any user with ordinary Deep Read — the secure record's children are not isolated at all (design.md:585-586, write-path registry I-2 "fail OPEN"); and nothing stops the next new writer from repeating it.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Dataverse/RecordOwnershipResolver.cs</output>
    <output type="code">the writer files listed in relevant-files</output>
    <output type="test">tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RecordOwnerAssignmentCensusTests.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/data-mutation/RecordOwnership/SecureChildOwnershipTests.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/unit/domain/Dataverse/RecordOwnershipResolverTests.cs</output>
    <output type="docs">docs/architecture/DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md</output>
    <output type="docs">projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-146-server-child-writers.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">The task note contains the census: every create/upsert/reparent site of a user-facing record in Sprk.Bff.Api, Spaarke.Dataverse and Spaarke.Core, each classified with file:line; every "fix" row is fixed in this task.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a child created by any fixed writer under a secure project, matter or work assignment, the row's `ownerid` is task 144's named team (integration test per writer family).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a child created under a non-secure parent, `ownerid` is the parent's BU default owner team — never the application user and never the caller unless no parent was named.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A `sprk_document` whose `sprk_relatedproject` points at a secure project and whose `sprk_project` points at an ordinary one is owned by the named team (secure-if-any).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: parent unreadable, owner ambiguous, or parent flagged `sprk_issecure = true` but not owned by the named team → nothing is created; the HTTP writers return a ProblemDetails with a stable reason code and a message (not a bare 500); background writers log and skip; the Secure BU default team is never assigned (fixture containing the default team as a decoy).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: a Dataverse fault during resolution propagates as the caller's 5xx and creates nothing; it is not reported as a refusal.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A reparent that moves a child under a secure parent reassigns it to the named team (read-back verified); one that moves it out of every secure parent reassigns it to the new parent's BU default team.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">An unfiled document save with an acting user and no related record still succeeds and is owned by the acting user's BU default team (required use case preserved).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">AUTHORIZATION: a contact's external reads and writes on children (documents, events, to-dos on the /api/v1/external surface) return the same results before and after — existing external suites green with no expectation changes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`RecordOwnerAssignmentCensusTests` fails when a seeded unowned app-only create of a listed entity is added, and passes on the final tree; every waiver carries a reason.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev, deployed with task 149): for each writer family, a child of a secure project, a secure matter and a secure work assignment is owned by the named team (read-back), and an existing non-admin test user with no share on the parent cannot open it in MDA (recorded); probes deleted and the deletion recorded.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md rows I-2 and I-6 state the new server owner and state.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BFF unit, integration and ArchTests green; publish size reported per CLAUDE.md §10 and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE; #1034 closed once with the outcome.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every child table the census classifies "fix" is present in the ONE codified set with its writer cited, and the live Secure Record Owner role holds Read on it in dev (recorded, per task 145's procedure) — no table is re-owned by code that Dataverse would refuse.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The census note classifies every content-bearing row type listed in the background (review logs, participant index rows, analysis outputs, email/attachment artifacts, thread and channel-ref rows) with a reason; none is left unclassified.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The PR description records the ship-together pairing with task 149 and the deployment ordering with task 152 (or the owner's recorded acceptance of the interim).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: the resolver rules and every writer family's secure/ordinary/refuse cases named above, plus the census seeding. Anything beyond needs a one-line justification in the task note.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Defect C10 half 2 (session26 note §C10; session27 note "C10 research" gaps 1 and 6, owner round 2 items 1, 6). Related: task 144 (named team + resolver secure branch), 145 (owner-role privileges — without them Dataverse refuses the assign), 147 (client writers), 148 (backfill/transitions — reuses this owner rule), 149 (internal sharees' access — ship-together), 150 (sprk_issecure lock), 152 (people-targeted notifications; same to-do/event writer files — serialize), 056 (contact coverage of children, amended). Peer: word-add-in-r1 task 080 / note 080 §6, PR #1045, #1034, #1043 (their fix — not redone here).</notes>

